hi there steve kaufman here today i'm  going to talk about  culture and language learning again if  you enjoy these um  discussions videos please subscribe to  my channel  the  subject oh and before i get started let  me say that today i've gone six weeks on  ukrainian and today i'm switching to  polish and you know what  the task that i put in front of me is to  work on four slavic languages  for the first four or five months of the  year to see uh to what extent it helps  me or it creates interference and stuff  like that and certainly going from  ukrainian to polish there's a lot of  similar vocabulary but so that's just to  update you now culture  and language learning  uh it's a subject that comes up all the  time  obviously when we  to effectively learn another language we  have to imitate  some aspects  of the cultural behavior  the behavior patterns of another culture  so  and i've always said that if we want to  speak  french well or chinese well or whatever  other language  we have to start thinking that we are  them and we have to  want to be part of them we want to  penetrate that world so inevitably we  want to  to some extent identify with that  culture  uh and you know even when i was learning  chinese  i think the counterpart of that is that  so much of what we find in another  culture in fact is universal so  therefore it's it's readily accessible  to us um you know  in the book that i wrote i don't have a  copy here about language learning uh on  the cover of the book i had this tree  which represented juan's tree the  parable of the crooked tree  drungzo  chinese  taoist taoist  philosopher  also has a very famous parable  where he says that he dreamt  and he dreamt that he was a butterfly  and when he woke up he didn't know if it  was a if it now he was a butterfly  dreaming that he was drunza or if that  was drunk's dreaming that he was a  butterfly and that's the kind of  call it cultural weightlessness  uh that we have to achieve in learning  another language so it's not just a  matter of being interested in the  culture  of the language we're learning we also  have to suppress  the interference from our own culture  uh you know i remember when i started  learning chinese for example i had  another canadian a diplomat studying  with me and when he found out that in  chinese to say you know are you going  to  that the chinese actually say you go not  go  it's not tremendously cultural but  that's how the language is structured  and his immediate reaction to that was  is that ever stupid  in other words he brought that back to  his own language his own culture so  that's different from my language  different from my culture therefore it's  stupid in fact you have to just allow  yourself to flow into that culture  now on the issue of culture  you know i think a lot of things in  these other cultures are  universal in their appeal otherwise we  wouldn't be interested in korean drama  russian novels um  you know french poetry whatever it might  be  and and all the great works of  literature wouldn't be translated into  50 languages these things all have  universal appeal we're all humans  uh they're all aspects that call it sort  of  human  creativity so they are tremendously  accessible but there are certain  specific call them idiosyncrasies of  certain  uh  languages cultures groups  and and not all of these are going to  appeal to us  and i sometimes think people who write  um textbooks spend too much time on  certain national festivals and uh very  sort of cultural idiosyncrasies that  that they're very proud of but may not  be of great interest to the learner uh i  find that i'm usually much more  interested in  how people interact around the dinner  table in the family at their place of  work in other words the living culture  of those people not  you know the harvest festival in korea  for example or flying carps in japan or  whatever it might be that that these  writers of textbooks always want to  present to us  so  i think culture we have to pick those  things that we like that we find  interesting  maybe there should be more freedom for  people to choose those aspects of the  culture if you're reading a history book  if you're reading a novel if you're  following korean drama you're  interacting with the culture and it  should be more up to the student to seek  out those things  that are interesting and for that matter  the same with people i often get the  comment well i was in paris and the  french were so silly and impolite and  stuff they're not all serially and  impolite but you don't have to like them  all you just have to like some you have  to find some friends so that you can  imagine yourself one of them  want to be one of them integrate into  their sphere even if it's only like a  droneza imagining himself a butterfly so  i think this issue of culture in  language learning it kind of goes both  ways  we  we need to  want to project ourselves into that  culture but we also need to have this  sense of  cultural weightlessness we need to leave  our culture you know aside take off that  coat  and try and put on this other coat so  there you have it there's a little  discussion uh on the issue of culture  and language learning and i look forward  to hearing your comments bye for now  [Music]  you
 

